Experiment No: 5

Create a Docker image for an application stored in local repository and run the application using Docker image.

Step 1:
Create a folder named “dockerimage” inside another folder named “Devops”.

Step 2:
Open the dockerimage folder in VS Code and create two new files:
sample.py
Dockerfile
Content of sample.py:
Python
print("Hello World")

Step 3:
Write the following content inside the Dockerfile:
Dockerfile
FROM python

WORKDIR /app

COPY . /app

CMD ["python", "sample.py"]

Step 4:
Open the terminal in VS Code inside the dockerimage directory and execute the following commands:
Run the Python file:
Bash
py sample.py
Build the Docker image:
Bash
docker build -t test.1 .
Here, test.1 is the image name.
Run the Docker container:
Bash
docker run --name cont1 test.1
Here, cont1 is the container name.

Step 5:
Open the Docker Desktop application and check whether the new image repository test.1 is added successfully or not.
You can also verify the running container named cont1 under the containers section.
Output:
Plain text
Hello World
This output is displayed after running:
Bash
docker run --name cont1 test.1

Step 6:
Copy the image tag by clicking on test.1 and copying the image tag shown in Docker Desktop.
Example:
Plain text
test.1:latest

Step 7:
Push the container image to a remote repository in Docker Hub using the following commands:
Tag the image:
Bash
docker image tag <copied_image_tag> <username>/test.1
Push the image to Docker Hub:
Bash
docker push <username>/test.1:latest

Step 8:
Check whether the container image is successfully pushed to Docker Hub by:
Opening Docker Desktop
Going to Docker Hub
Searching for:
Plain text
<username>/test.1

If the image appears in Docker Hub, then the push operation is successful.





Experiment No: 6

Create Docker Image using GitHub Repository and Build using Jenkins Pipeline

Step 1:
Go to GitHub and create a new repository with the name “dockerprog”.
Copy the repository URL and clone it using Git Bash with the following command:
Bash
git clone <your_repo_url>

Step 2:
Open the dockerprog folder in VS Code and create two new files:
Dockerfile
sample.py
Content of sample.py:
Python
print("Hello World")
Content of Dockerfile:
Dockerfile
FROM python

WORKDIR /app

COPY . /app

CMD ["python", "sample.py"]

Step 3:
Commit and push the changes to the GitHub repository using the following commands:
Bash
git add .
git commit -m "Docker project files added"
git push

Step 4:
Create a new Docker image and container by running the following commands in Git Bash.
Build the Docker image:
Bash
docker build -t test.2 .
Here, test.2 is the image name.
Run the container:
Bash
docker run --name cont2 test.2
Here, cont2 is the container name.
List all Docker images available in the local device:
Bash
docker images
Copy the IMAGE ID shown in the output and tag the image:
Bash
docker tag <image_id> <username>/test.2
Push the image to Docker Hub:
Bash
docker push <username>/test.2:latest

Step 5:
Open Jenkins and click on:
Settings
Plugins

Step 6:
Update all installed plugins.
Make sure the following plugins are installed:
CloudBees Docker Custom Build Environment
Docker
Docker Pipeline
Docker Compose Build Step

Step 7:
Create a new item in Jenkins with the name “Doc2” and select Pipeline as the item type.

Step 8:
Add the following pipeline script:
Groovy
pipeline
{
    agent any

    environment
    {
        dockerImage = 'ayusht05/test.2'
        registry = 'ayusht05/test.2'
        registryCredential = 'jenkin_docker_token'
    }

    stages
    {
        stage('Checkout')
        {
            steps
            {
                checkout scmGit(
                    branches: [[name: '*/main']],
                    extensions: [],
                    userRemoteConfigs: [[url: 'https://github.com/<username>/dockerprog.git']]
                )
            }
        }

        stage('Build Docker Image')
        {
            steps
            {
                script
                {
                    dockerImage = docker.build(registry)
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

Step 9:
Click on Save and then click the Build Now button on the left sidebar.
If the build is successful, Jenkins will display the build status with a success symbol.



